  Lint policy for every module. Pointed at by the shared lint block in the root
  build.gradle.kts, so this is the one place a rule is turned off - the same
  reasoning that keeps the locale gate in one script rather than one copy per
  module.
-->
<lint>

    <!--
      Scoped to the English regional overlays rather than disabled outright.

      values-en-rUS and values-en-rCA carry spelling-only differences and fall
      through to values/, which is neutral English by design. Lint does not
      model that fallback, so it reports every string in the source as
      untranslated for "en" - 734 errors in apps/market alone, none of them a
      real gap. Filling them would mean duplicating English under an English
      qualifier, which is the thing the design exists to avoid.

      The ignore matches the message rather than a path because lint reports
      these against values/strings.xml, the SOURCE file, so a path-scoped ignore
      cannot tell one locale's complaint from another's.

      Do NOT read the scoping as preserved coverage. Measured on this tree:
      deleting a real string from apps/market values-de and running lint with
      this ignore removed entirely produced the same 734 findings, all of them
      "en", and never mentioned German. MissingTranslation is not catching real
      locale gaps here, so the scope is precaution against a future lint that
      does, not a check anyone should rely on. tools/check-locales.py is what
      actually finds these - it caught that same deleted German string
      immediately, and failed the build before lint even ran.
    -->
    <issue id="MissingTranslation">
        <ignore regexp="is not translated in &quot;en&quot;" />
    </issue>

    <!--
      Fires on EVERY produceState call in the tree regardless of shape, including
      textbook ones whose only statement is an assignment to value. Confirmed
      against both call sites, and against three rewrites of one of them -
      hoisting the suspend call into a local, positional instead of named
      arguments, and dropping the `by` delegation - none of which it accepted.
      Compose BOM 2024.12.01; re-evaluate on a Compose upgrade.
    -->
    <issue id="ProduceStateDoesNotAssignValue" severity="ignore" />

</lint>
